{"meta":{"chapter":"03","section":"03","title":"Work-Energy for Rigid Bodies","course":"dynamics","learning_objectives":["Calculate rotational kinetic energy","Apply work-energy to rotating systems","Analyze rolling motion using energy","Combine translational and rotational energy"],"concept_ids":["rotational_kinetic_energy","rolling_motion","conservation_of_energy"],"physics_prereqs":["work_energy","rigid_body_kinetics"],"math_prereqs":["integrals"],"units_focus":["J","rad/s"],"common_errors":["rolling_constraint","energy_terms"],"estimated_time_minutes":50,"prereq_section_ids":["dynamics/03/02","dynamics/02/02"],"practice_ids":["rolling_speed_numeric","rotational_ke_numeric","rigid_body_total_ke_numeric","rolling_constraint_concept","rolling_energy_steps","translation_rotation_concept"]},"content":"# Work-Energy for Rigid Bodies\n\n> **\"A rolling or spinning body stores energy in both translation and rotation, and you lose accuracy if you forget either one.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n`work_energy` `rigid_body_kinetics`\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\n1. **Calculate** total kinetic energy for rigid bodies in plane motion\n2. **Apply** work-energy balances to rotational and rolling systems\n3. **Use** rolling constraints without double counting energy\n4. **Interpret** how inertia changes the final speed of a rigid body\n5. **Derive** the closed-form ramp speed and explain why shape (not mass) sets the answer\n\n---\n\n## Opening: The Energy a Spinning Mass Hides\n\nIn 2018, an industrial flywheel energy-storage unit in a frequency-regulation plant was rated to deliver a burst of power by trading rotational speed for electrical output. A steel rotor the size of a kitchen table, spinning in vacuum, was storing megajoules of kinetic energy in pure rotation, with its center of mass not moving at all. If you analyzed that rotor with the particle formula $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$, where $v$ of the center is zero, you would conclude it stored no energy. That conclusion would be catastrophically wrong, and it is exactly the kind of error this section exists to prevent.\n\nThe same blind spot shows up at the other extreme. A cask of wine and a hollow steel pipe of equal mass, released together down a ramp, do not arrive at the bottom together. A solid disk loses a race to a sliding hockey puck even on a frictionless-sliding comparison. None of this is mysterious once you accept one fact: **a rigid body in plane motion carries kinetic energy in two accounts, translation and rotation, and an honest energy balance must credit both.**\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Work-energy methods let you find a speed without ever solving for acceleration or contact forces. For rigid bodies the prize is even larger, because rotation often dominates the energy budget and force-moment analysis becomes tedious.\n\n---\n\n## Why Rigid-Body Energy Needs More Care\n\nIn particle dynamics, kinetic energy is just $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$. In rigid-body motion, a body can translate and rotate at the same time. That means one energy term is no longer enough.\n\nExamples:\n\n- a wheel rolling downhill\n- a flywheel storing energy in a drivetrain\n- a yo-yo translating downward while spinning faster\n- a robotic arm link sweeping while its center of mass moves\n\nIf you omit the rotational term, you generally predict a speed that is too high, because you have quietly assumed that all of the input energy went into linear motion.\n\n## Total Kinetic Energy of a Rigid Body\n\nFor general plane motion:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{T = \\frac{1}{2}mv_G^2 + \\frac{1}{2}I_G\\omega^2}\n$$\n\nThe two pieces mean:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}mv_G^2$: translational kinetic energy of the center of mass\n- $\\frac{1}{2}I_G\\omega^2$: rotational kinetic energy about the center of mass\n\nThis split is one of the central ideas of rigid-body dynamics. Here $v_G$ is the speed of the mass center, $\\omega$ is the angular speed, and $I_G$ is the mass moment of inertia taken about an axis through the mass center. The shape of the body enters only through $I_G$, which is why two bodies of identical mass can have very different energy bookkeeping.\n\n| Body (axis through center) | $I_G$ | $k^2 = I_G/(mr^2)$ |\n| --- | --- | ---: |\n| Thin hoop / ring | $mr^2$ | $1.0$ |\n| Solid disk / cylinder | $\\tfrac{1}{2}mr^2$ | $0.5$ |\n| Solid sphere | $\\tfrac{2}{5}mr^2$ | $0.4$ |\n| Thin spherical shell | $\\tfrac{2}{3}mr^2$ | $0.667$ |\n\n> **Key Insight:** The dimensionless ratio $I_G/(mr^2)$ measures how far the mass sits from the spin axis. The larger it is, the bigger the rotational share of the energy budget.\n\n## Rolling Without Slipping\n\nFor a rolling body,\n\n$$\n\\boxed{v_G = r\\omega}\n$$\n\nThis is a kinematic constraint, not a second energy term. It lets you rewrite the total kinetic energy in one variable, either $v_G$ or $\\omega$.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\nT = \\frac{1}{2}mv_G^2 + \\frac{1}{2}I_G\\omega^2\n$$\n\nwith $\\omega = \\frac{v_G}{r}$ becomes\n\n$$\nT = \\frac{1}{2}mv_G^2 + \\frac{1}{2}I_G\\left(\\frac{v_G}{r}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThat is why rolling bodies usually move more slowly than sliding particles descending the same height: some of the potential-energy drop becomes rotational kinetic energy.\n\n> **Golden Rule:** $v = r\\omega$ is a relationship you substitute, never a force or energy term you add. Add it as if it were an extra energy and you will double count.\n\n<dynamic-scenario\n  topic=\"Wheel Selection for a Delivery Robot\"\n  context=\"A robotics team must choose between solid aluminum wheels and lighter polymer wheels for a delivery robot descending a loading dock ramp. They care about downhill stability and want to predict how wheel inertia changes the bottom speed.\"\n  course=\"dynamics\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Work-Energy Balance\n\nThe same structure from particle dynamics still works:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{T_1 + V_1 + W_{nc} = T_2 + V_2}\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that $T$ now includes both translational and rotational pieces. A few practical notes for rigid bodies:\n\n- **Rolling friction does no work.** In pure rolling without slipping, the contact point has zero velocity, so the static friction force acts through zero displacement at each instant. It enforces the constraint without draining energy.\n- **A force applied at the mass center does work $\\vec{F}\\cdot \\vec{d}_G$**, using the displacement of the center.\n- **A couple moment $M$ does work $M\\,\\Delta\\theta$** through the angle it turns.\n- $W_{nc}$ collects only the genuinely non-conservative effects: bearing drag, hysteresis in a deforming tire, or slipping friction.\n\n## Derivation: The Universal Ramp-Speed Formula\n\nBefore working numbers, it pays to derive the result once in symbols so every rolling-body problem becomes a single substitution.\n\nStart from energy conservation for a body that starts at rest and rolls without slipping through a height drop $h$:\n\n$$\nmgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + \\frac{1}{2}I_G\\omega^2\n$$\n\nWrite the inertia in the compact form $I_G = k^2 m r^2$, where $k^2 = I_G/(mr^2)$ is the shape factor from the table above. Apply the rolling constraint $\\omega = v/r$:\n\n$$\nmgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\big(k^2 m r^2\\big)\\frac{v^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nThe $r^2$ cancels and every term carries $m$, so mass cancels too:\n\n$$\ngh = \\frac{1}{2}v^2\\big(1 + k^2\\big)\n$$\n\nSolving for $v$ gives the formula used throughout this section:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2gh}{1 + I_G/(mr^2)}}}\n$$\n\nTwo lessons fall out immediately. First, **mass and radius do not affect the final speed of a rolling body**; only the shape factor $k^2$ does. Second, a sliding particle is just the special case $k^2 = 0$, recovering $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$, the fastest any released body can reach for a given drop.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Universal Ramp Speed: v = sqrt( 2 g h / (1 + k2) )\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"h\",\"label\":\"Drop height\",\"min\":0.5,\"max\":5,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":2},{\"name\":\"k2\",\"label\":\"Shape factor I_G/(m r^2)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":1,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.5},{\"name\":\"g\",\"label\":\"Gravity\",\"min\":1.6,\"max\":9.81,\"step\":0.01,\"unit\":\"m/s^2\",\"default\":9.81}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Bottom speed v\",\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"expr\":\"sqrt(2*g*h/(1+k2))\"},{\"label\":\"Rotational energy fraction\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"k2/(1+k2)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"k2\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nSet `k2 = 0` to recover the sliding particle $\\sqrt{2gh}$, then slide it to `1.0` (a hoop) and watch the bottom speed fall while the rotational energy fraction climbs to one half.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Two wheels of the same mass and radius roll from the same height, but wheel A is a solid disk (k2 = 0.5) and wheel B is a thin hoop (k2 = 1.0). Which reaches the bottom faster?\" explanation=\"The bottom speed v = sqrt(2gh/(1+k2)) decreases as k2 grows. The disk (k2=0.5) gives v = sqrt(2gh/1.5), faster than the hoop (k2=1.0) at v = sqrt(2gh/2.0). Mass and radius cancel; only the shape factor matters.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The solid disk, because its smaller shape factor stores less energy in rotation\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The hoop, because all its mass is at the rim\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"They tie, because mass and radius are equal\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 3.3: Solid Disk Rolling Down a Ramp\n\n**Problem:** A solid disk with mass 5 kg and radius 0.2 m rolls without slipping from a vertical height of 2 m. Find its speed at the bottom. (Refer to a free body / energy diagram: weight $mg$ acting at the center, normal force and static friction at the contact point doing no net work.)\n\n**Step A: Write the initial and final energies**\n\n- Initial kinetic energy: $T_1 = 0$\n- Initial potential energy: $V_1 = mgh$\n- Final potential energy: $V_2 = 0$\n\nIf rolling without slipping and energy loss is neglected:\n\n$$\nmgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + \\frac{1}{2}I_G\\omega^2\n$$\n\n**Step B: Insert the disk inertia**\n\nFor a solid disk,\n\n$$\nI_G = \\frac{1}{2}mr^2\n$$\n\nUsing $v = r\\omega$:\n\n$$\nmgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}mr^2\\right)\\left(\\frac{v}{r}\\right)^2\n$$\n\n$$\nmgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + \\frac{1}{4}mv^2 = \\frac{3}{4}mv^2\n$$\n\n**Step C: Solve for speed**\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{\\frac{4gh}{3}}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $g = 9.81$ m/s$^2$ and $h = 2$ m:\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{\\frac{4(9.81)(2)}{3}} = \\boxed{5.11 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\nThe disk reaches the bottom more slowly than a frictionless sliding block would, because part of the gravitational energy becomes rotation. As a sanity check, the universal formula with $k^2 = 0.5$ gives $v = \\sqrt{2(9.81)(2)/1.5} = 5.11$ m/s, matching exactly.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Predict Each Move of the Energy Balance\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"For a solid disk rolling from rest, which energy accounts must the gravitational drop mgh split into?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Only translational kinetic energy (1/2) m v^2\",\"Translational plus rotational kinetic energy\",\"Translational kinetic energy plus rolling-friction heat\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"mgh = (1/2) m v^2 + (1/2) I_G omega^2. Pure rolling friction does no work, so all of mgh becomes kinetic energy split between translation and rotation.\"},{\"prompt\":\"After inserting I_G = (1/2) m r^2 and omega = v/r, what does the right-hand side collapse to?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"(1/2) m v^2\",\"(3/4) m v^2\",\"(5/4) m v^2\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"(1/2) m v^2 + (1/2)(1/2 m r^2)(v/r)^2 = (1/2) m v^2 + (1/4) m v^2 = (3/4) m v^2. The radius cancels and so does mass.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Solving mgh = (3/4) m v^2 for v with h = 2 m gives what bottom speed?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"4.43 m/s\",\"5.11 m/s\",\"6.26 m/s\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"v = sqrt(4 g h / 3) = sqrt(4*9.81*2/3) = 5.11 m/s. A sliding block (no rotation) would reach 6.26 m/s; the difference is the rotational energy cost.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Takeaway: substitute the rolling constraint v = r*omega into the energy terms, never add it as a separate term. Doing so once turns the two-variable balance into a single equation in v.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n### Compare Against a Sliding Particle\n\nIf the same object slid without rotation, the speed would come from\n\n$$\nmgh = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2gh} = \\boxed{6.26 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\nThat comparison is pedagogically useful. It makes the rotational energy cost visible: about $1 - (5.11/6.26)^2 \\approx 33\\%$ of the available energy went into spin rather than forward motion.\n\n---\n\n### Example 3.4: Energy Stored in a Flywheel (Pure Rotation)\n\n**Problem:** A solid steel flywheel of mass 40 kg and radius 0.30 m spins about its fixed central axis at 1500 rev/min. Its center of mass does not move. How much kinetic energy is stored?\n\n**Step A: Identify the energy terms**\n\nThe center of mass is stationary, so $v_G = 0$ and the translational term vanishes. All energy is rotational:\n\n$$\nT = \\frac{1}{2}I_G\\omega^2\n$$\n\nThis is the trap from the opening: the particle formula would report zero.\n\n**Step B: Compute the inertia and angular speed**\n\nFor a solid disk, $I_G = \\tfrac{1}{2}mr^2$:\n\n$$\nI_G = \\frac{1}{2}(40)(0.30)^2 = 1.80 \\text{ kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^2\n$$\n\nConvert the spin rate to rad/s:\n\n$$\n\\omega = 1500 \\times \\frac{2\\pi}{60} = 157.08 \\text{ rad/s}\n$$\n\n**Step C: Evaluate the stored energy**\n\n$$\nT = \\frac{1}{2}(1.80)(157.08)^2 = \\boxed{2.22 \\times 10^{4} \\text{ J}}\n$$\n\nAbout 22.2 kJ live entirely in rotation. To appreciate the scale, raising this same 40 kg mass would store $mgh$; the stored energy equals lifting it roughly $T/(mg) = 22{,}200/(40 \\times 9.81) \\approx 57$ m straight up. That is why high-speed flywheels are taken seriously as short-burst storage, and why containment is a safety requirement.\n\n---\n\n### Example 3.5: Cylinder Driven by a Horizontal Force (Work In, Speed Out)\n\n**Problem:** A solid cylinder of mass 8 kg and radius 0.25 m starts from rest. A constant horizontal force $P = 30$ N is applied at its center while it rolls without slipping on level ground. Find the center speed after the center has moved 4 m.\n\n**Step A: Account for the work**\n\nThe applied force acts at the center and moves with it, so it does work $P\\,d$. Static friction at the contact does no work in pure rolling. Gravity and the normal force are perpendicular to the horizontal motion, so they do no work either. The work-energy balance is therefore:\n\n$$\nP\\,d = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + \\frac{1}{2}I_G\\omega^2\n$$\n\n**Step B: Substitute the rolling cylinder energy**\n\nWith $I_G = \\tfrac{1}{2}mr^2$ and $\\omega = v/r$, the right side collapses (just as in Example 3.3) to $\\tfrac{3}{4}mv^2$:\n\n$$\nP\\,d = \\frac{3}{4}mv^2\n$$\n\n**Step C: Solve and evaluate**\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{\\frac{4P\\,d}{3m}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4(30)(4)}{3(8)}} = \\boxed{4.47 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\nCheck the energy ledger: work in is $P\\,d = (30)(4) = 120$ J, and $\\tfrac{3}{4}mv^2 = 0.75(8)(4.47)^2 = 120$ J. The books balance. Note that one third of the input work, 40 J, ended up as rotational kinetic energy even though no torque was applied directly; friction quietly converted some of the linear push into spin to keep the rolling constraint satisfied.\n\n## Quantitative Comparison Across Shapes\n\nThe same drop height does not produce the same final speed for every rolling body. Shape matters because inertia distribution matters.\n\nUsing the derived formula $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2gh}{1 + I_G/(mr^2)}}$ with $h = 2$ m:\n\n| Rolling Body | $I_G/(mr^2)$ | Final Speed |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Hoop | 1.0 | $4.43$ m/s |\n| Solid disk | 0.5 | $5.11$ m/s |\n| Solid sphere | 0.4 | $5.29$ m/s |\n| Sliding particle | 0.0 | $6.26$ m/s |\n\nThis is an important engineering comparison. A body with more inertia concentrated away from the axis stores more of the gravitational energy in rotation and therefore reaches a lower translational speed. The solid sphere wins every rolling race because it keeps its mass closest to the axis.\n\n## Comparative Test Mindset\n\nIf an engineer were selecting a wheel for a delivery robot, a simple comparison test might ask:\n\n- Which geometry gives the most stable downhill speed?\n- Which reaches the target speed with the least motor demand on level ground?\n- Which shape is more forgiving when the surface is slightly rough or when rolling losses increase?\n\nThat is where rigid-body energy becomes more than a classroom formula. It supports direct comparison among competing geometries before a prototype is even built.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Choose a Wheel for the Delivery Robot Descending the Ramp\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The robot must descend a loading-dock ramp with the most controllable (lowest) bottom speed for the same drop height. You can change only the wheel geometry, keeping mass and radius fixed. Which mass distribution do you specify?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Concentrate mass at the rim (hoop-like, k2 near 1.0)\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Good instinct for low speed: a larger shape factor stores more energy in rotation, leaving less translational speed.\"},{\"label\":\"Concentrate mass near the axle (solid-disk-like, k2 = 0.5)\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"This gives a faster, less controllable descent because less energy is diverted into rotation.\"},{\"label\":\"Make the wheel as light as possible\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Mass cancels in the rolling-from-rest speed, so lightening the wheel does not change the bottom speed at all.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"You picked the rim-loaded wheel. The test ramp then gets wet and the traction margin shrinks. What new risk dominates?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The rolling-without-slipping assumption may fail, so v = r*omega no longer holds\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Low traction can break the rolling constraint, dissipating energy as slipping-friction heat and invalidating the clean energy solution.\"},{\"label\":\"The wheel inertia suddenly changes\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"Inertia is a geometric property; it does not change with surface conditions.\"}]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"You picked the axle-loaded wheel for speed, but the spec actually wanted the LOWEST controllable speed. What is the consequence?\",\"feedback\":\"The robot arrives faster and is harder to stop on the dock. Re-read the design goal: a higher shape factor would have served the stability requirement better.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Lightening the wheel did not change bottom speed. What single quantity DOES set the rolling-from-rest speed?\",\"feedback\":\"Only the shape factor k2 = I_G/(m r^2) sets the bottom speed when a body rolls from rest. Mass and radius both cancel in v = sqrt(2gh/(1+k2)).\",\"choices\":[]},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"Correct design boundary identified. What is the right mitigation in the analysis?\",\"feedback\":\"Once slipping is possible, switch from the ideal rolling-energy model to one that includes kinetic-friction losses, or add traction margin so static friction keeps v = r*omega valid.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: inertia is fixed by geometry. What actually changed when the ramp got wet?\",\"feedback\":\"The available static friction dropped, threatening the rolling constraint, not the wheel inertia.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Sensitivity Analysis: Effect of Energy Losses\n\nThe ideal rolling examples above ignore non-conservative losses. Real wheels do not.\n\nFor the same 5 kg solid disk descending from a 2 m height, the balance becomes:\n\n$$\nmgh - E_{loss} = \\frac{3}{4}mv^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v = \\sqrt{\\frac{4(mgh - E_{loss})}{3m}}\n$$\n\nWith $mgh = (5)(9.81)(2) = 98.1$ J, the predicted speed changes as follows:\n\n| Energy Loss | Final Speed |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| 0 J | 5.11 m/s |\n| 5 J | 4.98 m/s |\n| 10 J | 4.85 m/s |\n| 20 J | 4.56 m/s |\n\nThis sensitivity view is often more useful than a single ideal answer. It shows how tolerant the design is to wheel quality, maintenance condition, or manufacturing variability. Notice the speed is fairly forgiving: losing one fifth of the available 98.1 J (20 J) costs only about 11% of the final speed, because speed scales with the square root of energy.\n\n## Assumption Breakdown Case: Rolling Without Slipping\n\nOne of the most important hidden assumptions in this section is that rolling without slipping actually holds.\n\nThat assumption can fail when:\n\n- the available static friction is too low,\n- a torque spike exceeds the traction limit,\n- the surface becomes wet, dusty, or compliant,\n- or braking loads exceed the contact's ability to transmit shear.\n\nOnce slipping begins, the clean relation $v = r\\omega$ no longer acts as a reliable kinematic constraint. Then:\n\n- static friction may be replaced by kinetic friction,\n- part of the energy is dissipated as heat,\n- and the simple rolling-energy solution can overpredict retained useful motion.\n\nThis is a genuine design boundary. A wheel concept that looks excellent under ideal rolling assumptions may behave very differently once traction margins shrink.\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | Why Rigid-Body Energy Matters |\n| --- | --- |\n| Flywheel energy storage | Stored energy is purely rotational; $\\tfrac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ sets capacity and burst power |\n| Vehicle wheels and rotating drivetrains | Rotational inertia adds an \"effective mass\" that the engine must accelerate |\n| Robot and rover mobility | Wheel inertia changes downhill speed, braking distance, and motor sizing |\n| Rolling mill and conveyor rollers | Start-up energy and coast-down time depend on $I_G$ |\n| Yo-yo despin and reaction wheels | Spacecraft trade rotation for translation through controlled energy exchange |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** Designers often quote the \"effective mass\" of a rolling wheel as $m(1 + k^2)$. A solid disk wheel behaves as if it were 50% heavier than its scale weight when you try to accelerate it, because the drivetrain must spin it up as well as move it forward.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- forgetting the rotational kinetic-energy term\n- using the wrong inertia formula for the shape\n- treating $v = r\\omega$ as optional in rolling problems\n- double counting the same motion by adding extra terms after already substituting the rolling constraint\n- charging energy to rolling friction (it does no work in pure rolling; only slipping friction and drag do)\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Why does a rolling rigid body usually reach the bottom of a ramp with less translational speed than a sliding particle descending the same height?\"\n  conceptId=\"rolling_motion\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because gravity is weaker for rigid bodies.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because some of the potential-energy drop is converted into rotational kinetic energy.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because mass cancels only for particles.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. In rolling motion, the body stores energy in both translation and rotation, so not all of the potential-energy drop becomes translational speed.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"For rolling without slipping, what is the role of v = r omega in an energy solution?\"\n  conceptId=\"energy_conservation_rigid\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"It adds a new force equation.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"It links translation and rotation so the energy equation can be written in one variable.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"It replaces the moment equation in every rigid-body problem.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Exactly. The rolling constraint is a kinematic relation. It helps express the translational and rotational kinetic-energy terms consistently.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Connecting the Concepts\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"rotational_kinetic_energy\",\"rolling_motion\",\"conservation_of_energy\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Total kinetic energy (plane motion) | $T = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv_G^2 + \\tfrac{1}{2}I_G\\omega^2$ |\n| Rolling constraint | $v_G = r\\omega$ |\n| Work-energy balance | $T_1 + V_1 + W_{nc} = T_2 + V_2$ |\n| Universal ramp speed (rolls from rest) | $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2gh}{1 + I_G/(mr^2)}}$ |\n| Work of a force / couple | $W = \\vec{F}\\cdot\\vec{d}_G$, $\\;W = M\\,\\Delta\\theta$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Rigid bodies store kinetic energy in translation and rotation.\n- Rolling constraints connect linear and angular motion; substitute them, never add them.\n- For a body rolling from rest, mass and radius cancel; only the shape factor $I_G/(mr^2)$ sets the speed.\n- Pure rolling friction does no work; only slipping, drag, and deformation dissipate energy.\n- Energy balances often solve rigid-body speed problems faster than force-moment equations.\n\n## What's Next?\n\nYou now have the three major problem-solving lenses for introductory dynamics:\n\n- force and acceleration\n- work and energy\n- impulse and momentum\n\nTogether they let you choose the representation that best fits the physics of the problem rather than forcing every problem into one method. The natural next step is **impulse-momentum for rigid bodies**, where angular impulse and angular momentum handle problems involving sudden impacts, spins, and collisions that energy methods alone cannot resolve cleanly.\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"rolling_speed_numeric","type":"numeric","stem":"A solid disk rolls without slipping from rest down a 1.5 m vertical drop. Find its speed at the bottom.","final_answer":{"value":4.43,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["rolling_motion","conservation_of_energy","rotational_kinetic_energy"]},{"id":"rotational_ke_numeric","type":"numeric","stem":"A rigid body has I_G = 0.6 kg*m^2 and angular speed 8 rad/s. Find its rotational kinetic energy.","final_answer":{"value":19.2,"unit":"J","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["rotational_kinetic_energy"]},{"id":"rigid_body_total_ke_numeric","type":"numeric","stem":"A 4 kg body moves with center-of-mass speed 3 m/s and has I_G = 0.5 kg*m^2 with omega = 6 rad/s. Find its total kinetic energy.","final_answer":{"value":27,"unit":"J","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["rotational_kinetic_energy","conservation_of_energy"]},{"id":"rolling_constraint_concept","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"What does the rolling-without-slipping relation v = r omega allow you to do in an energy solution?","options":["Ignore rotational energy","Replace translation with a force balance","Express both translational and rotational energy in one variable","Assume the body has zero acceleration"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Correct. The rolling constraint links the linear and angular motion so that the total kinetic energy can be written in terms of either v or omega.","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["rolling_motion","rotational_kinetic_energy"],"misconception_id":"rolling_constraint_unused"},{"id":"rolling_energy_steps","type":"step_based","stem":"A 3 kg hoop rolls without slipping down a 1.2 m height from rest. Find the final speed. Use I_G = m r^2 for a hoop.","steps":[{"step_id":1,"description":"Write the energy equation m g h = 1/2 m v^2 + 1/2 I_G omega^2.","expected_value":35.316,"unit":"J","tolerance":0.2},{"step_id":2,"description":"Substitute I_G = m r^2 and v = r omega to reduce the right side to m v^2.","expected_value":35.316,"unit":"J","tolerance":0.2},{"step_id":3,"description":"Solve for v.","expected_value":3.43,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1}],"final_answer":{"value":3.43,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["rolling_motion","conservation_of_energy","rotational_kinetic_energy"]},{"id":"translation_rotation_concept","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"Why does a rolling disk typically reach the bottom of a ramp more slowly than a sliding particle descending the same height?","options":["Because rolling bodies experience less gravity","Because some of the potential-energy drop becomes rotational kinetic energy","Because the disk has no translational kinetic energy","Because angular speed cancels gravitational effects"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Exactly. The rolling disk has to pay part of the energy budget into rotation, so the remaining translational speed is lower.","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["conservation_of_energy","rotational_kinetic_energy","rolling_motion"],"misconception_id":"missing_rotational_ke"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"missing_rotational_ke","pattern":"energy_term_omission","description":"Leaving out the rotational kinetic-energy term in a rigid-body energy balance","trigger":"learner uses T = 1/2 m v^2 only for a rolling or spinning body","feedback":"A rigid body can store kinetic energy in both translation and rotation. Use T = 1/2 m v_G^2 + 1/2 I_G omega^2 unless the motion is purely translational.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"rolling_constraint_unused","pattern":"constraint_error","description":"Failing to connect v and omega in a rolling-without-slipping problem","trigger":"learner leaves v and omega unrelated in the final energy equation","feedback":"Rolling without slipping provides the kinematic relation v = r omega. Use it to rewrite the energy equation in one unknown.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"wrong_inertia_model_shape","pattern":"shape_model_error","description":"Using the wrong moment of inertia formula for the body's geometry","trigger":"learner uses disk inertia for a hoop or hoop inertia for a disk","feedback":"Shape matters. Check whether the body is modeled as a hoop, disk, rod, or another geometry before substituting the inertia expression.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"double_counting_energy","pattern":"energy_accounting_error","description":"Adding extra kinetic-energy terms after already substituting the rolling constraint","trigger":"learner rewrites T in one variable and then appends an additional rotational or translational term","feedback":"Once you rewrite both kinetic-energy terms using the rolling relation, the expression already contains the full energy budget. Do not add another duplicate term.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"6214b34c3c370e7561d4230c27bd410ccb1b326952d9ed63f3a3e574cb098d37","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}